<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="photorespiration III" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.39|, commonly referred to as ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase or RubisCO, is a bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes both the carboxylation and oxygenation of |FRAME: D-RIBULOSE-15-P2| (RuBP). The carboxylation process is generally known as CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt; fixation (see |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|) whereas the oxygenation process is known as photorespiration (this pathway). The two substrates (CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) are competitive with regard to RubisCO, so that an increase in CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; concentration inhibits oxygenation and vice versa |CITS:[Bowyer97]|. An important factor influencing the rate of oxygenation is temperature, as photorespiration is enhanced by high temperature. The reasons for this are twofold: (i) as temperatures increase the solubility of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; diminishes more rapidly than that of O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and (ii) the specificity factor (&amp;Omega;) of RubisCO (which represents a measure of the relative specificity for CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) decreases with increasing temperatures between 7&amp;deg;C and 35&amp;deg;C. While the carboxylation reaction results in two molecules of |FRAME:G3P|, which are processed in the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|, the oxygenation reaction forms one molecule of |FRAME:G3P| and one molecule of |FRAME:CPD-67| (2PG). The latter cannot be used in the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY| and, instead, is salvaged in the photorespiration pathway. While photorespiration is essential for the viability of all organisms that perform oxygenic photosynthesis, including cyanobacteria and C3 plants, at ambient CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; conditions |CITS: [18957552][26994478]|, the photorespiratory cycle results in carbon, nitrogen and energy losses, and, unlike true respiration, does not produce ATP or reducing power. As a matter of fact, despite its name photorespiration does not involve neither light nor respiration. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The main features of the photorespiration pathway include: (i) the conversion of |FRAME: CPD-67| into |FRAME: GLY|; (ii) the processing of two glycines into |FRAME: SER|, CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and ammonia; and (iii) the conversion of |FRAME: SER| into |FRAME: G3P|, which is directed towards CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt; fixation via the |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY|. The first step of the pathway involves the dephosphorylation of |FRAME:CPD-67|, which not only recycles phosphate but also prevents accumulation of this compound, which is a potent inhibitor of |FRAME: EC-5.3.1.1|. The next step is the oxidation of |FRAME:GLYCOLLATE| to |FRAME:GLYOX|. This step is catalyzed by different enzymes in plants and some algae (see |FRAME: PWY-181|) and in chlorophytes and cyanobacteria (this pathway and |FRAME: PWY-8362|). Glyoxylate is converted into |FRAME:GLY| by two different enzymes: |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45| and |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.4|. One of the glycine molecues is converted by |FRAME: EC-1.4.1.27|, to CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, ammonia, and a single carbon that is transferred to tetrahydrofolate, forming |FRAME: METHYLENE-THF-GLU-N 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate|. The methylene group is then transferred to the second glycine molecule by |FRAME: EC-2.1.2.1|, forming |FRAME: SER|. The serine molecue feeds back into |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.45|, closing a cycle. The main carbon molecule produced by this cycle is |FRAME:OH-PYR|, which is reduced into |FRAME:GLYCERATE| by |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.29|. The pathway ends with the phosphorylation of glycerate to |FRAME:G3P|, which re-enters the Calvin cycle. The phosphorylation is performed by different enzymes in cyanobacteria vs. other photosynthetic organisms. The outcome of photorespiration in photosynthetic cells is loss of carbon via CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; release, as well as loss of energy, yet the pathway is essential at atmospheric concentrations of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. The biological function of photorespiration is not clear. One possibility is that photorespiration is necessary under conditions of high light intensity and low CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; concentration to dissipate excess ATP and NADPH from the photosynthesis light reactions, and prevent damage to the photosynthetic apparatus |CITS: [11299007]|. Another explanation is that the pathway is necessary for the assimilation of nitrate |CITS: [15272076]|. The only difference between this pathway variant, which exists in chlorophytes such as |FRAME: TAX-3055|, and the pathway variant in plants is that in plants and some algae |FRAME:GLYCOLLATE| is exported to the cytoplasm, where it is oxidized in the peroxisomes to |FRAME:GLYOX| by |FRAME: EC-1.1.3.15|. In chlorophytes (and cyanobacteria) glycolate is oxidized to glyoxylate by |FRAME: EC-1.1.99.14| |CITS: [18957552]|.</Description>
